# Trivy misconfiguration suppressions.
#
# Every entry here is a deliberate architectural decision, not an oversight.
# Findings that were genuine defects were FIXED rather than listed here:
#   * AVD-AWS-0164 - public subnets no longer auto-assign public IPs
#   * AVD-AWS-0095 - the SNS alarm topic is encrypted with a customer-managed
#                    KMS key whose policy lets CloudWatch publish to it

# AVD-AWS-0053: "Load balancer is exposed to the internet."
# The application is intentionally public - that is the assignment. The ALB is
# the only internet-facing component; the instances sit in private subnets.
AVD-AWS-0053

# AVD-AWS-0054: "Use of plain HTTP."
# No domain name or ACM certificate is in scope for this assignment, so the
# listener is HTTP/80. Production would add an HTTPS listener plus a redirect;
# the ALB module already accepts the change in a single block.
AVD-AWS-0054

# AVD-AWS-0104: "Security group rule allows unrestricted egress."
# The application instances must reach ECR, SSM, CloudWatch Logs and the
# Amazon Linux package mirrors, whose IP ranges are not stable. Egress is
# already restricted to ports 80 and 443, and all of it leaves through the NAT
# Gateway - there is no inbound path. The interface VPC endpoints defined in
# the security module are the route to tightening this further.
AVD-AWS-0104

# AVD-AWS-0132: "S3 encryption should use Customer Managed Keys."
# The Terraform state bucket uses SSE-S3 (AES256), which satisfies the
# "encryption enabled" requirement. A CMK would add key administration and
# cross-account policy overhead that this single-account assignment does not
# warrant. Bucket versioning, a TLS-only bucket policy and a full public
# access block are all in place.
AVD-AWS-0132
